        "headline": "Inside OpenAI's proof of\nthe Navier-Stokes problem",
        "body": "OpenAI's AI solution to the Navier-Stokes problem, and how it came about, hints at a chaotic future for math.",
        "mini_headline": "OpenAI's Navier-Stokes proof",
        "full": {
          "mode": "summary",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "t": "img",
              "src": "https://static.scientificamerican.com/dam/asset/676cb480-a9d0-4bb0-a42f-466e549cfa5c/ink-clash.jpg?m=1792683756.522&w=1200"
            },
            {
              "t": "p",
              "x": "On September 8, OpenAI claimed to have solved Navier-Stokes, one of the Clay Mathematics Institute's seven $1 million Millennium Prize Problems — right after N.Y.U. mathematician Tristan Buckmaster publicly accused the company of seeming to steal his work."
            },
            {
              "t": "p",
              "x": "Buckmaster had been working with Anthropic mathematician Levent Alpöge, prompting a mix of Anthropic and OpenAI models. On August 15 Claude blew up the Euler equations, and on August 22 the proof was verified in Lean."
            },
            {
              "t": "img",
              "src": "https://static.scientificamerican.com/dam/asset/f24c2ce2-1fe8-47ba-b469-5397351e6a59/lecture-collage.jpeg?m=1792685495.812&w=900",
              "cap": "Space was standing-room-only in Room 109 of N.Y.U.’s Warren Weaver Hall for Buckmaster’s first public lecture since his clash with OpenAI."
            },
            {
              "t": "p",
              "x": "Alarmed by rumors, OpenAI's team sent copies of a new internal model at the open Millennium Problems. A squad of 100 agents produced its own Euler proof; then a swarm of 10,000 agents, consuming a reported $15 million in computing, solved Navier-Stokes."
            },
            {
              "t": "img",
              "src": "https://static.scientificamerican.com/dam/asset/6a2f980d-9a8d-4ea5-8bfa-5220f1bf13f4/buckmaster-portrait.jpg?m=1792684575.161&w=900",
              "cap": "Tristan Buckmaster, a mathematician at N.Y.U.’s Courant Institute."
            },
            {
              "t": "p",
              "x": "Experts now say the swarm's Navier-Stokes proof took its own path. Buckmaster still believes OpenAI relied on his progress; OpenAI emphatically denies it. The episode warns that LLMs are proving results faster than humans can understand them."
            }
          ]
        },
